Finite-moment ferromagnetic bcc Fe regression for PBE, nspin=4, gga_grad=2.

The two-atom cubic cell has a lattice constant of approximately 2.87 Angstrom.
The body-centre atom is displaced from (0.5, 0.5, 0.5) to (0.503, 0.502, 0.499)
so all three atomic-force components are nonzero. Both initial moments are
(1.32, 1.056, 1.408) uB: a common tilted FM direction, not magnetic constraints.
No DeltaSpin, onsite projection, numerical orbitals, or SOC is enabled.
The converged total moment is approximately (2.70, 2.16, 2.88) uB/cell,
with magnitude 4.5 uB (cell-average 2.25 uB/Fe, not an atom-projected moment).

The existing Fe.upf contains PP_PSWFC data, allowing genuine atomic wavefunction
initialization. The previous Fe_ONCV_PBE-1.0.upf has no atomic wavefunctions and
would fall back to random initialization. No new pseudopotential is added.
The test uses 20 Ry, a 2x2x2 Gamma-centred k mesh, 40 spinor bands, Gaussian
smearing of 0.01 Ry, Davidson, and scf_thr=1e-10. The deliberately coarse basis
is for regression cost, not converged material energies, forces, or pressure.
In particular, the large coarse-basis pressure is not a physical bcc Fe result.

This replaces the previous near-zero-moment Fe2 fixture and its scf_thr=1e-14.
The new state is globally tilted and approximately collinear; it does not test
a spatially nonparallel spin texture. Weak-moment, clipping, local-rotation,
and density/metric-stress derivative coverage remains in the XC module tests.

From tests/01_PW, run:
  OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 bash ../integrate/Autotest.sh -a /path/to/abacus -n 4 -o 1 -r '^038_PW_NC_GGA_GRAD2$'

result.ref was generated with one CPU MPI rank, using -n 1 -g. CPU four-rank
and GPU one-rank runs pass against it with the unchanged default tolerances:
1e-7 eV for energy, 1e-4 eV/Angstrom for the force fingerprint, and 1e-3 kbar
for the stress fingerprint. Use device=gpu in a separate copy for the GPU run.
All three runs explicitly converge in 18 SCF steps. The full Autotest invocations
took 5.41, 3.67, and 7.69 seconds, respectively, on the tested V100 node (OMP=1).
These single-run times include launch/harness overhead, not queue/build time.

The force/stress fingerprints are sums of absolute components, not vector norms
or derivative-error bounds. Relative to CPU1, the largest fingerprint deviations
are 2e-6 eV/Angstrom and 1.7e-5 kbar. These are reproducibility checks, not new
end-to-end finite-difference validation. No test comparison tolerance is relaxed.
The reference must change because both the physical fixture and XC definition
have changed; it must not be compared directly with the retired Fe2 reference.
